facts:
A cyclic quadrilateral has all four vertices on the circle.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\draw (O) circle (2);
\coordinate (P) at (70:2);
\coordinate (Q) at (160:2);
\coordinate (R) at (235:2);
\coordinate (S) at (325:2);
\draw (P) -- (Q) -- (R) -- (S) -- cycle;
\fill (P) circle (1.5pt);
\fill (Q) circle (1.5pt);
\fill (R) circle (1.5pt);
\fill (S) circle (1.5pt);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Opposite angles of a cyclic quadrilateral are supplementary — they add to $180^{\circ}$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{angles, quotes}
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\draw (O) circle (2);
\coordinate (A) at (160:2);
\coordinate (B) at (70:2);
\coordinate (C) at (345:2);
\coordinate (D) at (240:2);
\draw (A) -- (B) -- (C) -- (D) -- cycle;
\pic[draw, "$a$", angle radius=0.45cm, angle eccentricity=2] {angle = D--A--B};
\pic[draw, "$b$", angle radius=0.45cm, angle eccentricity=2] {angle = A--B--C};
\pic[draw, "$c$", angle radius=0.45cm, angle eccentricity=2] {angle = B--C--D};
\pic[draw, "$d$", angle radius=0.45cm, angle eccentricity=2] {angle = C--D--A};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

An exterior angle of a cyclic quadrilateral equals the interior angle **opposite** its vertex, not the interior angle beside it.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{calc, angles, quotes}
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\draw (O) circle (2);
\coordinate (A) at (150:2);
\coordinate (B) at (70:2);
\coordinate (C) at (330:2);
\coordinate (D) at (230:2);
\coordinate (E) at ($(A)!-0.35!(D)$);
\draw (A) -- (B) -- (C) -- (D);
\draw (D) -- (E);
\node[below left] at (A) {$A$};
\node[above] at (B) {$B$};
\node[right] at (C) {$C$};
\node[below left] at (D) {$D$};
\node[above left] at (E) {$E$};
\pic[draw, "$x$", angle radius=0.45cm, angle eccentricity=2] {angle = B--A--E};
\pic[draw, "$x$", angle radius=0.45cm, angle eccentricity=2] {angle = B--C--D};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Move one vertex off the circle and the quadrilateral is not cyclic, so neither property holds.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\draw (O) circle (2);
\coordinate (P) at (70:2);
\coordinate (Q) at (160:2);
\coordinate (R) at (235:2.7);
\coordinate (S) at (325:2);
\draw (P) -- (Q) -- (R) -- (S) -- cycle;
\fill (P) circle (1.5pt);
\fill (Q) circle (1.5pt);
\fill (R) circle (1.5pt);
\fill (S) circle (1.5pt);
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

